@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+# ADR-0015: Read advisor master data from the ERP through the identity module
+
+- **Status:** Proposed
+- **Date:** 2026-11-04
+- **Deciders:** <to be filled by the reviewer>
+- **Sources:** T3-D-005, T3-AC-001, T3-D-009, T2-D-009, TD-15, ADR-0004
+
+## Context
+
+Head office needs the advisor master data that the ERP runs (discovery baseline section 2, DP1 section 3.1). The ERP port has only `readCustomer`. T3-D-005 and T3-AC-001 allow ERP access only through the `integration` module and its port. The person record with roles is owned by `identity` (T3-D-009), so `identity` is the module that callers ask for an advisor. Every ERP read records its source version and read time (T2-D-009). This adds a contract to the port (TD-15) and a dependency from `identity` on `integration`.
+
+## Options
+
+1. **New port operation `readAdvisor`, exposed by `identity.getAdvisor`.** Add an `AdvisorMasterData` Zod contract and `ErpPort.readAdvisor`; `integration.readAdvisor` returns an `ErpRead`; `identity` receives `Integration` as a dependency. Good: follows the ERP access conventions; callers do not know about the ERP. Bad: `identity` gains a dependency on `integration`.
+2. **Callers use `integration.readAdvisor` directly.** Good: no new dependency for `identity`. Bad: callers see ERP read metadata and ERP shapes; the advisor is a role of a person, which `identity` owns (T3-D-009).
+3. **A read copy (`erp_copy`) of all advisors, refreshed by a job.** Good: works when the ERP is down. Bad: needs the refresh design of TD-18 first; more than head office needs now.
+
+## Decision
+
+We choose option 1. It is the path the ERP access conventions describe, it keeps the ERP behind `integration`, and it puts the advisor read where the person roles live. A read copy (option 3) can replace the live read later without a change to `identity.getAdvisor`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `ErpPort` has a new operation `readAdvisor(advisorNumber)` and a new contract `AdvisorMasterData`: advisor number (`B` + 5 digits), given name, family name, e-mail, active flag, source version. The NAV adapter must implement it (TD-17).
+- `createIdentity` takes `integration` as a dependency. `integration` does not import `identity`, so there is no cycle; `pnpm arch` checks it.
+- `getAdvisor` is a live read. When the ERP is down it throws `ErpUnavailableError`; the caller decides the fallback (T2 section 5). The platform stores no copy of the advisor data, so no table and no data metadata are added.
